592186045417] [17592186157488 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157488 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157488 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157488 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157488 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157488 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157488 #edb/ref 1045 "Nonlinear Transformations of Plane Regions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157488 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186158042 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157488 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157488 #edb/ref 1050 1020.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157488 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "3a40bda5-f823-5841-986e-d65c61c1a55f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157614 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186153928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157614 #edb/ref 1069 "q-160610" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157614 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157614 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ and its **directed** boundary $∂Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, where $Δ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=v,\\,y=u-{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{3}$ under the action of $T$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157614 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "13842938-0c5a-56c1-b7e1-75af1a627153" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157614 #edb/ref 1074 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{3}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{3},$ we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(v,\\,u-{v}^{2}) \\\\  & =(1,\\,u-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =(1,\\,u-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{3}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=1,\\,y=u-1,\\,1\\ge u\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=1,\\,y(t)=t-1,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThis is a vertical line segment with endpoints $(1,0)$ and $(1,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/4c/4cda505c17437f64f8ab90dcccca1f55f087af77be6dee0e7c81dbd372760664.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157615 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186373896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157615 #edb/ref 1069 "q-160611" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157615 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157615 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx={u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,y=-v.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{2}$ under the action of $T?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157615 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5ebd6f32-1c51-561f-a98e-5d832c9928ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157615 #edb/ref 1074 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{2}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{2},$ we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,-v) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,-v) \\\\  & =({v}^{2}+1,\\,-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{2}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx={v}^{2}+1,\\,y=-v,\\,0\\le v\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+1,\\,y=-t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\nx=(-y)^{2}+1\\,⇒\\,{y}^{2}=x-1\n$$\n\nwhich is a right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(1,0).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{2}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(1,0)\\to ({1}^{2}+{0}^{2},-0)=(1,0) \\\\  & T:(1,1)\\to ({1}^{2}+{1}^{2},-1)=(2,-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{2}$ is an arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(2,-1).$\n\n![](images/ad/ade48b3f14ed83460a5d818f744103edd75f8394a3f3396a0ff30364bda36c9b.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158042 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158042 #edb/ref 1061 179739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158042 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0183c504-9dca-5d57-b6b2-7fbd845c0c1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158042 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158043 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158043 #edb/ref 1061 179740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158043 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "57f1d55f-1700-5d32-8bb5-972acd409176" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158043 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158044 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158044 #edb/ref 1061 179743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158044 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "50421ebe-c890-5ba5-a32a-9a94c89dfcb8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158044 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158045 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158045 #edb/ref 1061 179747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158045 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a6c347f5-faac-5cfe-bf7c-5e603e73acbf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158045 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158046 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158046 #edb/ref 1061 179750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158046 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f217b585-996f-5193-9dbe-40fa68d75ee1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158046 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158047 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158047 #edb/ref 1061 179751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158047 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "39d12d5d-e40b-5a0a-9a55-6c687ac4c3b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158047 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158048 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158048 #edb/ref 1061 179752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158048 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7ca8a633-49fc-5b82-89ef-24ced4348ee4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158048 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158049 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158049 #edb/ref 1061 179755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158049 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "658b558d-159e-5930-8d10-145b5fecb379" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158049 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158050 #edb/ref 1061 179756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158050 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "69d5e524-4c34-5148-a189-6502b171d912" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158050 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158605 #edb/ref 1064 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to find the images of simple objects in the plane under the action of nonlinear transformations.\n\nConsider the square region ![](images/47/479e96bb63311ab42547a0e2ee7a7d36ec21d0a868a1e356685ab871a486c3de.svg) and its boundary ![](images/1a/1a8826befbd05114d1886fbe8fd3f83b2e0bd868bcdedd76f0fd1350451f3bde.svg) in the ![](images/13/13377a2a85887e0a15df06f28ec7e6be35b98579a0865d0292a7f64da0d57b2e.svg)-plane, where ![](images/47/479e96bb63311ab42547a0e2ee7a7d36ec21d0a868a1e356685ab871a486c3de.svg) is given by\n\n![](images/f2/f285f8016e64c5ab750d94dac618fe2c1a09ced6f5fce7bbaea85e7258eda2c5.svg)\n\nA sketch of this region, specified with a counterclockwise orientation, is shown below.\n\n![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\n     \n\nSince ![](images/1a/1a8826befbd05114d1886fbe8fd3f83b2e0bd868bcdedd76f0fd1350451f3bde.svg) is specified with an orientation, we refer to it as a **directed boundary.**\n\nNow, consider the transformation ![](images/0c/0cedb44f040d579cf90349a6891c23cb11a0cc35833979b96447815746831d35.svg) defined by\n\n![](images/a3/a3c2f6f5a3103e4fb023f9f433ce5fa33a215d23f5a33c8bf82b21b852e1236c.svg)\n\nwhere \n\n![](images/d5/d58f2d66646ec6255b8de257d9940476f78ea04fb65a8e0db6d19663399f0efa.svg)\n\nOur task is to find the image of ![](images/47/479e96bb63311ab42547a0e2ee7a7d36ec21d0a868a1e356685ab871a486c3de.svg) under the action of ![](images/43/43a304a55ccd9c3f567bc9ca58aa2fb6b9aa0f8847f74a7c3dbbab3519729bb3.svg) \n\nFirst, note the following:\n\n- It's easy to check that ![](images/ee/eefe57a16ee4b7baeb91597e3e1f25ed4fa583bffef387058ee3d4c563ae7809.svg) has no critical points in the *interior* of ![](images/47/479e96bb63311ab42547a0e2ee7a7d36ec21d0a868a1e356685ab871a486c3de.svg) (we'll discuss why this matters shortly).\n\n- Since ![](images/47/479e96bb63311ab42547a0e2ee7a7d36ec21d0a868a1e356685ab871a486c3de.svg)'s interior contains no critical points, we only need to find the image of its boundary ![](images/7d/7d7b652d219e930e1989bd99378c234b82ecbb8aa5eaec0574539267f3f70946.svg)\n\n- To find the image of ![](images/85/85bc8c8e3680298e08b6aecd3c4a66dcfb72f96ef1433d1bccb464af8311aee0.svg) we parametrize each *directed* edge and map them individually onto the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane.\n\nLet's now map each directed edge of ![](images/1a/1a8826befbd05114d1886fbe8fd3f83b2e0bd868bcdedd76f0fd1350451f3bde.svg) to the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ![](images/37/3796e37203fb4ff84036acfaea5f94257c722a75b9b4896885548a54bd1e15ab.svg) where we have ![](images/a9/a983770395cdbf5053f0d2b8e7118ae80bbb9aba8f12def26afe08b3608ca39b.svg) and ![](images/23/233735103d4322c64d03fcb3c3c36b2968737ee86c5932190541afa9b0bf9c58.svg) Substituting ![](images/a9/a983770395cdbf5053f0d2b8e7118ae80bbb9aba8f12def26afe08b3608ca39b.svg) into our transformation equations, we obtain\n![](images/26/26d77a4baefb6a2d4a17264f5f8e7b9922e99cdedd62e6bfe2b08aba99ef0d17.svg)\nSo, the image of ![](images/0e/0e765225794b08faeff45f99be1f19d1e37c33b0a0584e6cefa164e0b865d979.svg) can be parametrized by\n![](images/35/3567fd4fae25e9a36d2a48b68dec31311753e8cec3d13b0657db727124eaae3d.svg)\nWriting these parametric equations in the usual notation, we have\n![](images/4f/4fd68f3aee9087b1c0f8b86a57fae2035fa8527d5f0b74038d7c3339e8724457.svg)\nThese are parametric equations of a line segment. Moreover, the endpoints of ![](images/0e/0e765225794b08faeff45f99be1f19d1e37c33b0a0584e6cefa164e0b865d979.svg) are mapped to the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane as follows:\n![](images/46/468ae20a0d581d780de7cb0af719f5a34e3141c44700b0e27353df12cb001bfe.svg)\nTherefore, the image of ![](images/0e/0e765225794b08faeff45f99be1f19d1e37c33b0a0584e6cefa164e0b865d979.svg) is a line segment traversed from the point ![](images/11/11fcd48b1c7ad209409abaa1d91d8c374286782f2f426108d0ef29ee47602ad4.svg) to ![](images/fd/fd83db97b0675d3527a9f2a7a68d38a98f2d05ab5b1771d961c0d687e44c191b.svg)\n\n![](images/13/132642571953ddbfc95c3f0c399cd3c612a2ba2a84a224939b6f40e7d22a074f.png)\n\n- Consider the image of the side ![](images/24/24d3419f793c33397116d086271facafc3e7dc955e696abc2edbd24b307d2d6e.svg) Here, we have ![](images/5d/5d6b28a28a071240c3dc689d764fabcbbc2b3ad5a27491457b455e007260a69d.svg) and ![](images/7c/7cebf80c26d37190278268fc56dc3b471c289f9f9b5112adac1210a972789142.svg) Substituting ![](images/5d/5d6b28a28a071240c3dc689d764fabcbbc2b3ad5a27491457b455e007260a69d.svg) into our transformation equations, we obtain\n![](images/41/41118e0a120a0a6df82567ce1925c2b4d21b8f6c50a1c8c927382361d4ab089a.svg)\nThis horizontal line segment is traversed from the point ![](images/42/42f562f132f08838fe095cdeb883c38dd65e0d77828f65f73d783980c52f34ec.svg) to the point ![](images/e2/e2002597cf3d8b01e24e7b4d2c6daf6fc9afb0afb3c6140877abfc3bf1be02c4.svg) in the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane.\n\n![](images/13/131644a2410e5127a69388f1bd1fced1e28698e7634b137fcd47f8b3f26121a3.png)\n\n- Consider the image of the side ![](images/c4/c4229f343b9b55013188ec3d2755c4f2b0517098764a1217d19ae440c0a93987.svg) Here, we have ![](images/28/2818d199c13addae115e475e4fd88c1e86f5990ad506cc6193576ec948a5b155.svg) and ![](images/80/8065c8e9bcaad0d0173e6a81ba9da342ceae55c13883b6ae9c9141c595b8bb7f.svg) (we write the inequality for ![](images/46/4620fefc72ebf96810ce9f348c9a728f392246ff87133b6d0e5549607fde421d.svg) in this way to show that ![](images/f4/f4d449c4e8f90acd89fab00620e798faf115df672253f2eb4a840c7b52610be8.svg) is traversed *from* the point where ![](images/5d/5d6b28a28a071240c3dc689d764fabcbbc2b3ad5a27491457b455e007260a69d.svg) *to* the point where ![](images/b0/b02fba4dd5cb988b97123113f936580578350a063e3c8bcfe498d08b8bdc6fa4.svg)).\n\nSubstituting ![](images/28/2818d199c13addae115e475e4fd88c1e86f5990ad506cc6193576ec948a5b155.svg) into our transformation equations, we obtain\n![](images/7e/7ea2885f9400b3908c7dfc98f22b094126c44142421b444b156e44f42e210762.svg)\nSo, the image of ![](images/f4/f4d449c4e8f90acd89fab00620e798faf115df672253f2eb4a840c7b52610be8.svg) can be parametrized by\n![](images/98/9838e3c4e7e9493167069fcdd079eb2137c6b163497626d42aa6e2283233996e.svg)\nWriting these in the usual notation, we have\n![](images/e4/e4ac0ebf10c9c6d9c69ce2ae73031bf89e3262351a9129fecd6e9225af2d9dd4.svg)\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate ![](images/70/707bb132def3690d81bf1f0c8cf416c1382322ab19d66264cdab1decc4669307.svg) from the equation, we get\n![](images/d4/d488c4edbe9302b258749eb38ec9b104dcaab55c5eb63a88fcff9b00625b6b3b.svg)\nwhich is a right-opening parabola whose vertex is at ![](images/ab/ab6b2f25fb90ef8bea8b21d9d0253752c37bb3c77b675547a223b03f97d3bf6a.svg) Moreover, the endpoints of ![](images/f4/f4d449c4e8f90acd89fab00620e798faf115df672253f2eb4a840c7b52610be8.svg) are mapped to the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane as follows:\n![](images/05/0537b85fecac45a4548b9907713fb5cbb1bafaeda9f7aa89f712c6e89cb739f8.svg)\nTherefore, the image of ![](images/f4/f4d449c4e8f90acd89fab00620e798faf115df672253f2eb4a840c7b52610be8.svg) is an arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from the point ![](images/e2/e2002597cf3d8b01e24e7b4d2c6daf6fc9afb0afb3c6140877abfc3bf1be02c4.svg) to ![](images/ab/ab6b2f25fb90ef8bea8b21d9d0253752c37bb3c77b675547a223b03f97d3bf6a.svg)\n\n![](images/bb/bb73b7c44c9fec45c3c3a632eed03db6f07db3ebbaff9e8e3941d22e78f478db.png)\n\n- Consider the image of the side ![](images/7a/7a8b22dcfbad030cb2ef985aa65b6051afcd75ee410eabdf7e88f931ccb53d81.svg) Here, we have ![](images/b0/b02fba4dd5cb988b97123113f936580578350a063e3c8bcfe498d08b8bdc6fa4.svg) and ![](images/2b/2b2a85d8c09127a2a022c9a63d1c522661922f621a2f3005c877b7c490ca359e.svg) Substituting ![](images/b0/b02fba4dd5cb988b97123113f936580578350a063e3c8bcfe498d08b8bdc6fa4.svg) into our transformation equations, we obtain\n![](images/d6/d62c96cc3ca6e3993cfcf5d5ab2833922e0c6e80689ba8c8dc80e4186ad6cac4.svg) \nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point ![](images/dc/dcc19bac01833ff23365244490e52b2722741da56e804e9a95b5b264f23216bf.svg) to the point ![](images/11/11fcd48b1c7ad209409abaa1d91d8c374286782f2f426108d0ef29ee47602ad4.svg) in the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane.\n\n![](images/7c/7c72a5f4341ab24df69d1375588abd655abbe8fec77ecd41e52501b602b7e3fb.png)\n\n      \n\nAnd that's it! We have successfully computed the image of ![](images/47/479e96bb63311ab42547a0e2ee7a7d36ec21d0a868a1e356685ab871a486c3de.svg) in the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane. \n\nIt's highly recommended that you redo this calculation yourself. Make sure you can construct the image of ![](images/13/13e2e6cea8ac23c623cd84a4d1d2ea58e3072ddcc5e8fc720cbe3e91ebff8fbe.svg) including its orientation (note that the image of ![](images/47/479e96bb63311ab42547a0e2ee7a7d36ec21d0a868a1e356685ab871a486c3de.svg) is traversed *clockwise*)." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158605 #edb/ref 1065 9449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158605 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5c011b18-998e-546d-a4cc-736249313138" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158605 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158606 #edb/ref 1064 "![](images/74/74e8a3822098768743be16f0af09d4365d424c83ea473eb56160f4c5f15c5531.png)\n\n        \n\nEarlier, we found that the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to \\big( v(u^2+1), \\: u \\big) = \\big( u^2v+v, \\: u \\big) ,\n$$\n\nmaps the square region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane shown above. \n\nWe deduced the image of $\\Delta$ by mapping each edge of its boundary. For this method to work, we need to check that $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points in the *interior* of $\\Delta.$ \n\nTo check for critical points, we start by computing the Jacobian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFor the partial derivatives of $x(u,v),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}  = \\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial u}(u^2v+v) = 2uv, \\qquad  \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial v} =  \\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial v}(u^2v+v) = u^2+1,\n$$\n\nand for the partial derivatives of $y(u,v),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}  = \\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial u}(u) = 1, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}  = \\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial v}(u) = 0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our partial derivatives into our expression for the Jacobian, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}2uv & {u}^{2}+1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(1+{u}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, note the following:\n\n- The Jacobian is negative everywhere in the $(u,v)$ plane. So, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n- Since the Jacobian is negative everywhere, the transformation $\\mathbf T$ is orientation-reversing everywhere (note, for example, that $\\Delta$ and $D$ have opposite orientation).\n\nTo apply this method, we require that $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points in the *interior* of $\\Delta.$ This method works even if the *boundary* of $\\Delta$ contains critical points. Let's explore this a little more." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158606 #edb/ref 1065 9450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158606 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "40f68814-34bc-5d92-b1f0-78ce6045757d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158606 #edb/ref 1067 "The Significance of Critical Points" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158607 #edb/ref 1064 "![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\n  \n\nLet's again consider our square region $\\Delta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\Delta  = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nNow consider the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to \\big( uv, \\: u^2+uv) \\big).\n$$\n\nThe Jacobian matrix determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}v & u \\\\ 2u+v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSetting the Jacobian equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\n-2u^2 = 0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad u = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the critical points of $\\mathbf{T}$ lie on the line $u = 0.$ This coincides with the side $S_4$ of our square.\n\n![](images/6e/6ea454e2a95bf5a9bef1d585c1620ff78a98586c2500293cdfd136c7562a5f83.png)\n\n  \n\nNow, since there are no critical points in the interior of $\\Delta,$ we can find its image in the usual way. However, the side $S_4$ may collapse to a single point. So, let's go ahead and finish this example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158607 #edb/ref 1065 9451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158607 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "af247fbf-2e40-5486-964b-7b1effb2c06f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158607 #edb/ref 1067 "Critical Points on the Boundary of the Domain" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158608 #edb/ref 1064 "Until now, we've specified that our transformation $\\mathbf T$ should not contain critical points in the interior of the preimage $\\Delta.$ So let's briefly discuss what to do when $\\Delta$ contains critical points.\n\nOnce again, let's consider the region \n\n$$\n\\Delta  = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nin the $uv$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\n             \n\nWe now consider the image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to \\big( uv, \\: u^2+v^2 \\big).\n$$\n\nLet's start by finding the critical points. First, we compute the Jacobian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}v & u \\\\ 2u & 2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2{v}^{2}-2{u}^{2} \\\\  & =2(v+u)(v-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe critical points correspond to the solutions of \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x,y)}{\\partial (u,v)} = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the critical points are given by\n\n$$\n2(v+u)(v-u) = 0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad u=\\pm v.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the critical points of $\\mathbf T$ that lie *inside* $\\Delta$ are located on the line $u = v.$\n\n![](images/af/af0cb392b9194a37c7f7e29455e066be52b28e11c8486edc8c992a82d3df625a.png)\n\n             \n\nTo find the image of $\\Delta$ in this case, we divide $\\Delta$ into two regions $\\Delta_1$ and $\\Delta_2$ along the critical line (as shown below) and compute their images separately.\n\n![](images/96/965c24ef46882d40d4ff71b30b2ff60f891298a5dc48a267afc946f719e2b245.png)\n\n     \n\nLet's compute the images of these two regions:\n\n- Computing the image of $\\Delta_1$'s boundary, we find that \n\n- $\\mathbf{T}(S_1)$ is a vertical line segment traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(0,1),$\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}(S_2)$ is an upward-opening parabola traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(1,2),$ and\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}(C_1)$ is a line segment traversed from $(1,2)$ to $(0,0).$\n\n![](images/c7/c79e3dfcc15a6f5f7c77c59e99d67a29103e77a550a7c53a98b6868d786f5925.png)\n\n     \n\n- Similarly, computing the image of $\\Delta_2$'s boundary, we find that \n\n- $\\mathbf{T}(C_2),$ a line segment traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,2),$\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}(S_3),$ an upward-opening parabola traversed from $(1,2)$ to $(0,1),$ and\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}(S_4),$ a vertical line segment traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(0,0).$\n\n![](images/eb/ebeae2b6425c15fb5670fc83dc64f07a2d407fd108d26c51d8f301d979732cdb.png)\n\n     \n\nSuperimposing the two results, we obtain the following image of $\\Delta{:}$\n\n![](images/28/28993d9cff3621f5b1633940c161aa528ef2b143bafca967cb55bdd9ffa4e1e0.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- As $(u,v)$ runs over $\\Delta$ once, its image $D$ is traced out twice.\n\n- The boundary of $D$ that runs from $(0,0)$ to $(1,2)$ is the image of the critical line $u=v,$ which is *not* a bounding curve of our original region $\\Delta.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158608 #edb/ref 1065 9452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158608 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8817e0ca-4c4f-5e82-b97a-2f4e6047eab4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158608 #edb/ref 1067 "Critical Points Inside the Domain" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158609 #edb/ref 1064 "We'll now justify why the Jacobian determinant gives the (local) area scale factor of a transformation.\n\nConsider a rectangular region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane of length $\\delta u,$ width $\\delta v,$ whose bottom-left corner is at the point $(u_0,v_0),$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/9a/9a7d700c343fd2807e70b43d688c2f47ed3724e88fc9849e23aa20ee0be10b15.png)\n\nFurthermore, we assume the following:\n\n- $\\mathbf T: \\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow \\mathbb R^2$ is a $C^1$ transformation throughout $\\Delta,$ and contains no critical points.\n\n- The image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ is the region $D.$\n\n- The image of $(u_0,v_0)$ is $(x_0,y_0).$\n\n- The image of $S_1$ is $S_1',$ and its endpoints are $(x_0,y_0)$ and $A.$\n\n- The image of $S_2$ is $S_2',$ and its endpoints are $(x_0,y_0)$ and $B.$\n\nSince $S_1'$ is the image of $S_1,$ it can be parametrized by varying $u$ only. Similarly, since $S_2'$ is the image of $S_2,$ it can be parametrized by varying $v$ only. So, the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively, are given by\n\n![](images/1b/1be386cd85ad48bcc75b4d292ebeeee0d8af0bf8df2297696b9384c448130bb3.svg)\n\nNow, since $\\delta u$ is small, $S_1'$ can be approximated by a line segment. Similarly, since $\\delta v$ is small, $S_2'$ can also be approximated by a line segment. Let's construct two vectors $\\mathbf{s}_1$ and $\\mathbf{s}_2$ that span our two segments, as shown below.\n\n![](images/1f/1ff10cdcde237698a28c6f76053de50745ef12c67dfc5290ac6e06893d201972.png)\n\nWe can find the vector $\\mathbf s_1$ by subtracting the position vectors of its endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{1}=a-[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{0} \\\\ {y}_{0}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}x\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0}) \\\\ y\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}x\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0}) \\\\ y\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}x\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})-x\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0}) \\\\ y\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})-y\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0})\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that when $\\delta u$ is small, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{∂x}{∂u}|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})} & ≈\\frac{x\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})-x\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0})}{\\delta u} \\\\ {\\frac{∂y}{∂u}|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})} & ≈\\frac{y\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})-y\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0})}{\\delta u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector $\\mathbf s_1$ can be approximated as follows:\n\n![](images/b7/b766b50b744af71539daaf12c1e32e44f493ac71bbabb8b387c78bb8092886b8.svg)\n\nUsing similar arguments, we have the following approximation for $\\mathbf s_2{:}$\n\n![](images/f3/f3203536e63ab67521014fe9e8d623cf00535ae8d35adee9b6405253d532dcfc.svg)\n\nThe area of $D$ is approximately equal to the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\mathbf s_1$ and $\\mathbf s_2.$ Therefore,\n\n![](images/55/55a8a36b0617400f963430eb1ba31ef20cbddeb4f09eaeaa8920fae39b4b145e.svg)\n\nSubstituting our approximations for $\\mathbf s_1$ and $\\mathbf s_2,$ we arrive at the desired result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ≈{|det[\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\delta u\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\,\\,\\delta v\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂v}\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\delta u\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\delta v\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\,\\,\\end{aligned}]|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})} \\\\  & ={|\\delta u\\delta v\\cdot (\\frac{∂x}{∂u}\\frac{∂y}{∂v}-\\frac{∂x}{∂v}\\frac{∂y}{∂u})|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})} \\\\  & =\\delta u\\delta v\\cdot {|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158609 #edb/ref 1065 9453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158609 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "07200f56-2408-51a2-9e3d-6b5d727ef5ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158609 #edb/ref 1067 "The Jacobian as a Local Area Scale Factor" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288084 #edb/ref 1080 "$(0,-1)$ and $(2,0)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288084 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288084 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "06977182-5497-5c11-a57f-add4aeca5aa7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288228 #edb/ref 1080 "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(-1,-1)$ to $(1,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288228 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288228 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1e97e49e-99a8-5167-95a0-4c3a2aa2b214" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288296 #edb/ref 1080 "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(-1,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288296 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288296 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "27c2c113-8813-50c2-98ea-35f094997ebe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288347 #edb/ref 1080 "$(0,0)$ and $(2,-1)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288347 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288347 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2ece2530-2ca7-5ec1-a8cf-c55ac6d46052" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288404 #edb/ref 1080 "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(-1,0)$ to $(0,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288404 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288404 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "370f9d77-be30-5124-88d9-5efd241282bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288667 #edb/ref 1080 "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(-1,-1)$ to $(0,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288667 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288667 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5b0618de-1ff4-56f